)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120370 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t) = (1 - t)i + e^{t}j$ at $t = 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a657ab49-8316-5498-aff1-401071c8812e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120601 #edb/ref 1061 11439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "402c2124-8063-56c7-bb37-ea2cdcc0a66c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120602 #edb/ref 1061 11440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71cffa1e-47f4-55d9-a319-71cf51f73286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120603 #edb/ref 1061 11441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6ca150c-baf4-5085-bb7d-62a05a15bc30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120604 #edb/ref 1061 11444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2fbbaa6-20ed-599e-ba35-826ad87a33df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120605 #edb/ref 1061 11445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a177d231-4807-586b-8e78-fdd08a9a7171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120606 #edb/ref 1061 11449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c7bc743-0c19-5cb7-bd3d-35d253cf54f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121011 #edb/ref 1064 "For a plane curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthe *curvature* of $C$ at an arbitrary point can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the **signed curvature** $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t) = \\dfrac{x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe signed curvature tells us whether the curve bends up, down, or straight:\n\n* if [math]k > 0,[/math] then the curve bends upward (i.e., it is concave up)\n\n* if [math]k < 0,[/math] then the curve bends downward (i.e., it is concave up)\n\n* if [math]k=0,[/math] then the curve is straight.\n\nFor example, consider a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 1+t^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the curvature of this curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{'}(t)=2,\\, & {y}^{'}(t)=2t \\\\  & {x}^{''}(t)=0,\\, & {y}^{''}(t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our curvature formula, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{[{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,4\\,|}{{2}^{3}{[1+{t}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t =1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{1}{2{({1}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121011 #edb/ref 1065 2534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121011 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b40dd6c7-fe0a-5126-9014-ec7c0d5eb29b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121011 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121012 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the following formula for unsigned curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nTo see where this formula comes from, remember that for a curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),  y(t),  z(t)\\rangle,$ the unsigned curvature formula is\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$\n\nIf a curve does not have a $z$-component, then we may consider $z(t) = 0.$ Thus, any plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\:  y(t), \\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\langle x'(t), y'(t),0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf r''(t) = \\langle x''(t), y''(y),0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our cross-product formula, we find that the curvature $\\kappa(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨{x}^{'}(t),{y}^{'}(t),0⟩\\times ⟨{x}^{''}(t),{y}^{''}(t),0⟩‖}{‖⟨{x}^{'}(t),{y}^{'}(t)⟩{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨0,0,{x}^{'}(t){y}^{''}(t)-{y}^{'}(t){x}^{''}(t)⟩‖}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{'}(t)^{2}+{y}^{'}(t)^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{'}(t){y}^{''}(t)-{y}^{'}(t){x}^{''}(t)|}{({x}^{'}(t)^{2}+{y}^{'}(t)^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121012 #edb/ref 1065 2535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16d4e0f1-81e9-5252-9390-52f2e8512d48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121012 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Curvature of a Plane Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121013 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a plane curve defined by the explicit equation \n\n$$\ny = y(x).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can parametrize this curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=t,$ we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{'}(t) & ={y}^{'}(x) \\\\ {x}^{''}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{''}(t) & ={y}^{''}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the (unsigned) curvature, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|{x}^{'}(t){y}^{''}(t)-{y}^{'}(t){x}^{''}(t)|}{([{x}^{'}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{'}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot {y}^{''}(x)-{y}^{'}(x)\\cdot 0|}{({1}^{2}+[{y}^{'}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{y}^{''}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{'}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unsigned curvature for $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (x) = \\dfrac{|y''(x)|}{ (1 + [y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nand the signed curvature is\n\n$$\nk(x) = \\dfrac {y''(x)} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\t.\n$$\n\nNote that the signed curvature $k(x)$ has the same sign as $y''(x).$ Therefore,\n\n- if $k(x) > 0,$ the curve is concave up, and\n\n- if $k(x) < 0,$ the curve is concave down." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121013 #edb/ref 1065 2536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "66d1756a-304e-51f3-9622-3074a6e63c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218