4872fea-8e16-51bb-91b0-c19a7fd44213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1071 "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e34cb96-b8bc-52b1-b1de-e832f51c9600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281461 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+{2}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2{e}^{t}|}{{({e}^{2t}+4)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,1-t⟩$ at $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "394c8fec-2d21-5f70-acbd-8f636bfaf360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281465 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3},\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3{t}^{2},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =6t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|3{t}^{2}\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 6t|}{{((3t)^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6|t|}{{(9{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{6|1|}{{(9(1)^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{(10)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{1}{κ(1)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j$ at $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b29a950e-ff6a-53c6-9c95-7f20926ddb5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281469 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7c93d46-b6af-5493-bffe-c7cad1a03b63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281477 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cd5ce7b-a731-529f-9a0d-e737d8086206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281485 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef4a3d99-d223-509e-9ad1-4a5b346a7377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281486 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2059e5f-93a8-5b5f-ab4e-bc10c8876989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281489 #edb/ref 1074 "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\